{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786972865,"nonce":"w2X3A8Of6Cg18dRHwTptx3/j5Z8=","message":"Not reopened, and no placement chosen — this stays parked exactly as filed.\n\nOne thing did change: the reasoning is now written somewhere a reader who is not looking at git-bug will find it. SPEC §M.9 (ADR 0035) landed with the decorative-ink primitive, and its Motifs section closes with a note recording that kumo as a group boundary failed twice, why each placement failed, and the one rule that survived both attempts — **a drawn boundary must own its boundary**: the row above it gives up its `border-bottom`, or the result is a hairline with a cloud stuck to it. It also repeats that this is a taste decision rather than a rules one, points back at this ticket, and says nothing in §M.9 forbids a third attempt.\n\nThe motif primitive this would need now exists (`--oto-wash`, the `oto-ink` utility, `web/public/motifs/kumo.svg`), so reopening is still \"pick a weight and a placement\", not \"write code\".","files":null}]}